[Event "San Sebastian"]
[Site "San Sebastian ESP"]
[Date "1911.??.??"]
[White "Rubinstein, Akiba"]
[Black "Capablanca, Jose Raul"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D33"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense: Rubinstein System"]
[Annotator "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals (1921); lichess study by frankv53"]
[Source "https://lichess.org/study/MLvmSIiK/XWNsh0yH"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. c4 e6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. g3 Be6 { 6...Nf6 is the normal move in this variation. White's development was first introduced by Schlechter and elaborated later on by Rubinstein. It aims at the isolation of Black's d-pawn, against which the White pieces are gradually concentrated. In making the text move I was trying to avoid the beaten track. Being a developing move there should be no objection to it in the way of general principles, except that the Knights ought to come out before the Bishops. } 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O Rc8 { In pursuance of the idea of changing the normal course of this variation, but with very poor success. The move in theory ought to be unsound, since Black's King's Knight is yet undeveloped. I had not yet learned of the attack founded on Ng5 and the exchange of the Bishop at e6. Either ...Nf6 or ...h6; to prevent either Bg5 or Ng5, was right. } 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Ng5 Nf6 11. Nxe6 fxe6 12. Bh3 Qe7 13. Bg5 O-O { This is a mistake. The right move was 13...Rd8 in order to get the Rook away from the line of the Bishop at h3 and at the same time to support the d-pawn. Incidentally it shows that White failed to take proper advantage of Black's weak opening moves. Against the text move White makes a very fine combination which I had seen, but which I thought could be defeated. } (13... Rd8) 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 { I considered 14...gxf6, which it seemed would give me a playable game, but I thought White's combination unsound and therefore let him play it, to my lasting regret. } (14... gxf6) 15. Nxd5! Qh6 16. Kg2! { This is the move which I had not considered. I thought that Rubinstein would have to play 16 Bg2, when I had in mind the following winning combination: 16 Bg2, Ne5! 17 Nf4 (if 17 Rc1, Qxc1!!; 18 Qxc1, Bxf2+ wins), ...Ng4; 18 h3 (if 18 Nh3, Bxf2+ wins the exchange), ...Nxf2; 19 Rxf2, Bxf2; 20 Kxf2, g5, and Black should win. It is curious that this combination has been overlooked. It has been taken for granted that I did not see the 17th move Qc1. } (16. Bg2 Ne5 17. Nf4 (17. Rc1 Qxc1 18. Qxc1 Bxf2+) 17... Ng4 18. h3 (18. Nh3 Bxf2+) 18... Nxf2 19. Rxf2 Bxf2+ 20. Kxf2 g5) 16... Rcd8 { After White's last move there was nothing for me to do but submit to the inevitable. } 17. Qc1! exd5 18. Qxc5 Qd2 19. Qb5 Nd4 20. Qd3 Qxd3 21. exd3 Rfe8 22. Bg4 { This gives Black a chance. He should have played 22 Rfe1. If then 22...Nc2; 23 Rxe8+, Rxe8; 24 Rc1, Re2; 25 Kf1, Nd4 (if 25...Rd2; 26 Be6+, Kf8; 27 Bxd5 would win); 26 Rc8+, Kf7; 27 Rc7+, Re7; 28 Rc5 wins. } (22. Rfe1 Nc2 23. Rxe8+ Rxe8 24. Rc1 Re2 25. Kf1 Nd4 (25... Rd2 26. Be6+ Kf8 27. Bxd5) 26. Rc8+ Kf7 27. Rc7+ Re7 28. Rc5) 22... Rd6 23. Rfe1 Rxe1 24. Rxe1 Rb6 25. Re5 Rxb2 26. Rxd5 Nc6 27. Be6+ Kf8 28. Rf5+ Ke8 29. Bf7+ Kd7 30. Bc4 a6 { A bad move, which gives away any legitimate chance Black had to draw. It loses a very important move. In fact, as the course of the game will show, it loses several moves. The proper way was to play 30...Kd6. If then 31 Rb5, Rxb5; 32 Bxb5, Nd4; followed by ...b5; and White would have an exceedingly difficult game to draw on account of the dominating position of the Knight at d4 in conjunction with the extra Pawn on the Queen's side and the awkward position of White's King. (See how this is so.) } (30... Kd6 31. Rb5 Rxb5 32. Bxb5 Nd4) 31. Rf7+ Kd6 32. Rxg7 b5 33. Bg8 a5 34. Rxh7 a4 35. h4 b4 36. Rh6+ Kc5 37. Rh5+ Kb6 38. Bd5 { With these last three moves White again gives Black a chance. Even before the last moveBc4 would have won with comparative ease, but the text move is a downright blunder, of which, fortunately for him, Black does not avail himself. } 38... b3 { 38...Rxa2 would make it practically impossible for White to win, if he can win at all. White's best continuation then would have been: 39 Bc4, Rc2; 40 Rb5+, Kc7; 41 Bg8, a3; 42 h5, a2; 43 Bxa2, Rxa2, and if there is a win it is very difficult to find it, as against 44 h6, Ra6! offers excellent chances for a draw. } (38... Rxa2 39. Bc4 Rc2 40. Rb5+ Kc7 41. Bg8 a3 42. h5 a2 43. Bxa2 Rxa2 44. h6 Ra6) 39. axb3 a3 40. Bxc6 Rxb3 { If 40...a2; 41 Rb5+, Ka6; 42 Rb8. } (40... a2 41. Rb5+ Ka6 42. Rb8) 41. Bd5 a2 42. Rh6+ { Black resigns.
As an end game, this is rather a sad exhibition for two masters. The redeeming feature of the game is Rubinstein's fine combination in the middle game, beginning with 14 Bxf6. } 1-0
